  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Impact Cluster: Greenport Morocco</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Protected horticultural production is one of the most important economic activities of the region of Agadir, Morocco. Conditions are particularly favourable to tomato production but the region faces constraints that limit sustainable growth.Climate change results in the decline of regional surface water reserves and the need for increasingly deeper wells indicates a dropping ground water table. The quality and age of the greenhouses cause poor productivity and quality, high level of post-harvest losses, and poor resistance to pathologies and weather shocks. These elements threaten winter production, which is the most valuable given the &apos;export window&apos; to the European Union (EU). There is a need to introduce more sustainable ways of production and increase productivity, quality and reliability.Morocco and The Netherlands joined forces in 2015 by initiating plans to set up a Centre of Excellence on Horticulture in Agadir. The main goal was to strengthen the relationship between Morocco and The Netherlands in the field of research and higher education and strengthen Moroccan capacity in covered horticulture techniques and technology while creating business opportunities for Dutch companies. A demonstration greenhouse was jointly financed and constructed during 2022.This Impact Cluster enables the Netherlands to continue its involvement through activities around the Centre of Excellence and contribute to knowledge exchange and local adaptation of technological solutions.An increase of yields and production levels, and less water-consuming and resilient growth techniques are necessary and feasible, even within restrictive business models for local growers. For this purpose, the impact cluster will set up a broad range of activities including demonstration, training and coaching, and study groups to target low-tech and mid-tech farmers with the aim to increase their knowledge and adoption of new technologies. Together, these efforts support the region in a transition to become a sustainable protective horticulture hub.</narrative>
  </description>
